WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.339
Lovely!

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:11.469
I’ve just realised that erbium is the most
crucial element for the internet

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:16.369
of all the elements on the periodic table,
and so I want to take you

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:20.590
across to materials engineering where we can
see a demonstration which

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:25.020
will explain to you why it’s so important,
and why without erbium many

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.810
of you couldn’t be watching this video.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:32.390
Erbium is another lanthanide metal. It’s
soft, silvery, quite malleable,

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:38.950
very reactive towards oxygen and water and,
I suppose, for us right now

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.510
it’s quite significant, because it was originally
discovered from a mineral

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:48.100
which was found in this quarry. So we’re
in a quarry in Sweden near

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:54.360
Stockholm, called Ytterby Mine and some black
minerals that were found

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.730
in this quarry actually were the birthplace
for four new chemical

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.870
elements and erbium was one of them.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:06.860
Erbium is a photographic filter and it is
actually a nuclear poison. What

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:13.000
that means is that it will kill dead any nuclear
fission process so it’s

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.909
obviously got some applications there. It’s
an interesting element when

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:20.060
you make compounds of it and dissolve it in
solution it often goes pink.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:27.750
Now this is some erbium trichloride. You’ll
notice it’s very slightly pink. I

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:31.069
think this is one of the ones that, because
of its pink colourations, get’s

00:01:31.069 --> 00:01:36.029
used in jewellery and things like sunglasses
and things like that, if

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:37.899
memory serves.

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:44.200
More recently erbium is used as an amplifier
of light as it’s transmitted

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.930
down optical fibres.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:51.889
So, the reason we are here, and why I’m
all dressed up, these are the

00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:57.520
fibres that transmit the signals for the internet.
Now the reason they use

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.299
fibres is because copper cables which you
would normally use for

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:07.130
electrical signals cannot carry nearly as
much information, as many

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:12.650
YouTube viewings at once, as one of these
fibres. And the fibre is very

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:17.969
strong, neither Brady nor I have managed to
break it, and the problem

00:02:17.969 --> 00:02:23.170
is that even with this fibre, with the light
beam going down it, eventually

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.560
the light gets weaker and weaker and you lose
the signal. So every

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:35.220
50km, every 30 miles or so, down the fibre
when it’s under the sea you

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.049
have to amplify the signal to get it back
up again so that our YouTube

00:02:40.049 --> 00:02:44.740
video can reach the other side of the Atlantic
or to India or wherever.

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:48.700
And so the way you’ve got to do it you’ve
got to somehow amplify the

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:53.640
light, make it more intense, and you can do
this with erbium because if

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:00.250
you put erbium into the glass then if you
shine a light onto the erbium

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:07.469
atoms they get excited and then as the light
comes in from the signal it

00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:13.651
interacts with the erbium atom and the erbium
atom loses its energy and

00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:17.840
gives out more light. So essentially the light
comes in and comes out

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.980
twice as intense. It then keeps going another
30 miles and you repeat it.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.329
Here we’ve got the infrared laser, I used
to work with lasers, but I’ve

00:03:27.329 --> 00:03:34.030
rather given up now. So it’s quite nostalgic.
Is it switched on? And if you

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:38.719
put this in the laser beam you can see there’s
a special screen here,

00:03:38.719 --> 00:03:44.349
which will actually take an infrared laser
beam which you can’t see and

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:49.969
make it into visible light so you can see
it. We’ve got infrared light going

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:55.230
into a piece of glass with erbium in it. Now,
what is happening here is the

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.450
physics here is slightly different from what
happens in the amplifier, but

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:03.930
is really quite fun because the light beam
that you can’t see is suddenly

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:07.139
being changed into visible light, to green
light, which you can.

00:04:07.139 --> 00:04:11.900
Yes it’s very important, I had heard about
it in lectures but when we

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:17.389
videoed our first video about erbium I had
completely forgotten about

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:21.340
this, although without it nobody would have
seen any of our videos.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.530
Welders, people that join pieces of metal
together using either large

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:30.120
electric fields or extreme temperatures have
special masks to protect

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.560
their eyes from the very bright light which
is given out in the welding

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.960
process. Welding goggles typically can include
erbium salts because they

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:42.000
are intense and they absorb a massive amount
of the light so the eyes of

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.199
the welder are not damaged at all.

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:50.250
So, under the sea you have these fibres with
copper wires going next to

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:55.340
it carrying the currents for the diodes that
excite the amplifiers all the

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:02.050
way along and, apparently, the current going
down these wires is quite

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:08.330
attractive to sharks and so in shallow water
sharks come along and see

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:13.250
the cable and it seems attractive and they
start chewing it and, although

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:21.270
Brady and I can’t break the fibres, a shark
in full spate can easily cut

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:26.680
through it and so there have been quite alot
of problems with the optic

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.460
fibres in shallow water being chewed to bits
by sharks.

